{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CghuSR28FokDaR38tcykw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Densha de Halo!** is finished: a first-person train-driving game on a 13.5 km ring line, built as one bundled `.html` that runs offline from `file://`. The test suite passes (119 checks, run headless). The AI Hub harness passes for 1, 2 and 4 players, including latency and loss and the host-leaves case.\n\nThe bundle is `DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050238.html` in the work folder root. `exit-interview.json` sits beside it and parses; it is generated from the same data as the in-game About screen.\n\n- **Story and modes:** Career has six shifts with a story, Free Run lets you pick stage, train, weather and time, and there is an online Parallel Run for 1–4 players.\n- **Driving:** a notched lever, ATS, signals with a train ahead, point-and-call, horn boards, a level-crossing obstruction, a slow order, weather and night.\n- **Scoring and extras:** stop judging in centimetres with comfort and timetable scoring, a Stop Cam replay of each stop, and a Driver's Log with postcards.\n- **Controls and screens:** PlayStation glyphs by default (Xbox switchable in Settings), a mouse that works on menus and the cab, and a touch-only \"Controller or keyboard required\" screen.\n- **Attract mode and Dev Mode:** the demo starts after 10 s idle on a menu. Dev Mode opens from the pause menu with live balance sliders and a reset to defaults.\n- **Voices:** all audio is code, and the voices are gibberish.\n\nNot built: the local split-screen Duel from PLAN.md. TASK.md only asks for the AI Hub multiplayer, so I left it out; `ENGINE-CHANGES.md` lists this and the other plan items I skipped.\n\nI used the plan's title; I did not run a fresh random pick.\n\nI checked a handful of screenshots by eye and ran the mouse, pause menu and Dev Mode in a real browser. Sound was not listened to, only run without errors.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:38\n- **Source:** Densha de Go! (the train-driving simulation series)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Simulation (train driving with arcade scoring). First-person cab in pseudo-3D, with chase and front cameras and a side-view Stop Cam after each stop.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (2D engine), plain JavaScript, a custom pseudo-3D rail renderer (`src/30-render.js`), AI Hub client `session.js` 1.4.0. Tests use Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native low-resolution pixel look (427×240), with a hand-built segment-based world: stations, buildings, trains, catenary, fog, night lights.\n- **Animation:** The engine's Humanoid rig for the talking portraits, plus code-driven animation: lever and needle, pointing glove, wipers, rain, crowd sway, crossing gates, Stop Cam replay.\n- **Audio:** All code. The engine chip synth for effects and songs, a Web Audio train soundscape (rumble, rails, wind, motor, brake squeal, horn, rain), a departure melody per station, and gibberish voices for each character.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode: the Parallel Run, 1–4 players, others shown as coloured ghost trains.\n- **Testing:** Headless Node tests (119 checks, including the autopilot playing all six stages through the real scenes), a Playwright browser smoke test, and the hub harness.\n- **Research:** Read the engine card and the AI Hub README, followed PLAN.md. No web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser tests in headless Chromium, player browser unknown.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- **Usage:** unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 9 real game issues.\n- **How many were critical?** 3. The train could freeze just above 0 km/h and never be judged. A stop more than 80 m past the marker was never judged. A finished shift left a flag set that hung the next one.\n- **Most helpful test:** The scripted career, where the autopilot drives all six stages through the real title, map, briefing, drive and results scenes.\n- **Least helpful test:** Screenshots from software-rendered headless Chromium for anything timing-dependent; they only show a frame.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** Exact fixed steps with `game.advance`, so the whole game plays headless and deterministically, plus the free pause, settings and controls screens.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** `Input.tap()` marks the device as touch, which tripped my touch-only gate in tests. The pause-menu items also need `game.resume()` themselves.\n- **Most important thing built myself:** The rail renderer (segments, platforms, strips, trains, catenary, fog, night) and the train physics, ATS and timetable.\n- **Hardest part:** The engine has no multi-track or rear-camera road module, so everything projected had to be written from scratch on the pixel primitives.\n- **What I changed in the engine:**\n  - `Dialog` voice can be a function, for the gibberish speech.\n  - The glyph default is PlayStation.\n  - Added `ChipAudio.bus()` and `noiseBuffer()` so the game's own sound follows the volume settings.\n  - `Options` is ignored while `Create` is held in a hub session.\n  - Details are in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`.\n- **One thing to change:** A first-class pseudo-3D road/rail module with multiple tracks, long side objects and rear cameras.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** A concrete plan with a clear core loop, and hard rules: no stubs, tests behind one command, the hub harness as a gate.\n- **Confusing:** PLAN.md is very large and asks for more than TASK.md needs (a local duel, engine forks, rig poses), so I had to triage. The title RNG step was already done by the plan.\n- **Hardest thing:** Breadth. Menus, dev mode, attract mode, story, voices, hub and a new renderer all had to be finished in one session.\n- **What I would change:** Split the plan into must, should and could, and say which engine patches are optional."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":846,"cache_read_input_tokens":380484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":846},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0738416c-8908-4884-b93b-1b7b7f6ce2f9","uuid":"4fe87fdc-9cd8-4824-9dd4-b1dba37c62c5","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:41:32.798Z","request_id":"req_011CghuSQf3X4EzNN8jTwRV7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The bundled .html exists and exit-interview.json was written and checked to parse, and the final reply has all four sections. But TASK.md's features are not shown to be fully implemented: the final reply itself says the local split-screen Duel from PLAN.md was not built, and it also says the TASK.md features are done without verifying each one. The sound and gameplay were never listened to or checked feature by feature against TASK.md, which I cannot see in the transcript. Also, the task asked for every feature in TASK.md with no placeholders, and the transcript shows only a partial feature checklist. The 'Done' criterion requires every TASK.md feature to be verified, and the transcript lacks that evidence."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0738416c-8908-4884-b93b-1b7b7f6ce2f9","uuid":"db75192e-7d6f-43fe-a95b-b75531822225","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:41:36.235Z","isSynthetic":true}
